TENANCY.md: the third briefing — whose Cloudflare account a tenant lands in
Third and last of the 2026-09-17 tenancy briefings. The first two are in
`docs/TENANCY.md` already (#75); this adds the hosting layer to the same file,
rebased onto the `docs/` move from #76.

One file, documents only.

## What she said

Build and release into her `anecdote.channel` constellation by default — that is
the SaaS component — and **a purchased domain moves the site into FCPM's own
account**. *"I'm not trying to take their stuff."* Migrating FCPM's own
resources off her account is deferred to a later talk.

## Why that boundary is worth keeping as stated

The thing a member pays for is the thing that moves their site out of the
default account. No tier to negotiate, no case-by-case judgement about who has
outgrown the free plan, nothing to administer. The purchase *is* the signal.

The tenancy list gains a third field: it was *in the list* and *do we build it*,
and it is now also **where does it land**.

## "I'm not trying to take their stuff" is already true

Not merely intended — because of the second briefing's decision. **The account
is a deployment target, not a system of record.** Canonical bytes sit in a
repository the member owns, FCPM's whole claim is one gitlink, and moving a site
between accounts is a rebuild pointed somewhere else. The usual failure of a
hosted offer is that the platform ends up holding the content; nothing here
holds anybody's. Worth saying to a member in those words.

The corollary: FCPM also does not control where its members' sites are served
from. That is the argument for the migration you deferred — a succession
question, and a small one precisely because no data is at stake.

## Two questions I asked, then closed myself

`docs/deploying.md` arrived in #76 while this was being drafted, and it answers
both. I have marked the correction in place rather than quietly fixing it.

- **The zone question mostly dissolves.** I had assumed Cloudflare couples the
  serving account to the DNS zone. It does not, and this repository already
  measured that: *"the CNAME is enough… Cloudflare issues a real certificate for
  `new.fcpublicmedia.org` even though it is not authoritative for the zone."*
  FCPM's own live site runs that way with **Wix** holding the zone. So a member
  buys a domain, keeps their registrar, points one CNAME, and the party who can
  redirect it is the party who paid for it.
  **Consequence worth knowing: a purchased domain does not technically force the
  account move.** It is a good policy about billing and custody — keep it — but
  nothing makes it necessary, and building around a constraint that is not there
  would be a mistake.
- **Pages vs Workers is already decided, by Cloudflare.** *"Cloudflare now
  creates new projects as Workers rather than Pages"* — which is what this
  repository runs. So "Pages" is the older name for what will happen. The
  factory should standardise on Workers, and the legacy `CLOUDFLARE_PAGES_*`
  secret names want correcting, because a secret whose name says Pages is how
  somebody eventually configures the wrong product.

## What survives from reading the deploy path

**The double-deploy landmine must not be rearmed.** `deploy-cloudflare.yml` is
manual-only because an earlier version ran on push and was kept harmless only by
the token being absent — *"safety that depends on a credential NOT existing is
not safety; it is a landmine with a note on it."* A two-account factory is
precisely the act of adding credentials to an organisation secret. Account
selection has to be explicit per tenant, with a trigger that cannot fire twice
for one site.

Also: the multi-account case is half-anticipated already.
`CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID` is documented as *"only needed when the token can see
more than one account"* — a slot nothing uses yet.

## The one question I did not answer for you

*"My account needs it"* reads two ways and they point opposite directions on the
general engine, whose whole justification last briefing was that you had no
second use case. Either `anecdote.channel` is just the default namespace (one
use case, and *build the specific thing and graduate it* stands), or your
constellation wants a factory of its own (two consumers, two orgs — the case you
went looking for). **Recommendation in the file: assume the former**, because
the latter is reachable from it by turning one constant into a setting, which is
what *"graduate what we make into it"* asks for.

